[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Making Real API Requests with cy.request() in Cypress"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Cypress is primarily known for end-to-end browser testing, it also possesses powerful capabilities for interacting with servers directly via HTTP. The cy.request() command allows you to make AJAX requests, seed databases, check backend health, and handle authentication tokens without interacting with the DOM."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use cy.request()?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Speed: Bypassing the UI to perform actions like login or data setup significantly speeds up your test suite."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Setup and Teardown: You can easily seed test data before running a test and clean it up afterward."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"API Testing: Validate backend endpoints independently of the frontend application."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic GET Request"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The simplest use case for cy.request() is fetching data from an endpoint using the HTTP GET method. By default, it resolves with an object containing properties like status, body, headers, and duration."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"it("Makes a simple GET request", () => {
  cy.request("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1")
    .its("status")
    .should("equal", 200);
});"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"POST Request with Payload and Headers"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To send data to a server, pass an options object containing the method, url, body, and any required headers. Cypress automatically serializes JavaScript objects into JSON strings and sets the Content-Type header accordingly."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"it("Makes a POST request to create a resource", () => {
  cy.request({
    method: "POST",
    url: "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts",
    body: {
      title: "Cypress API Testing",
      body: "Learning how to use cy.request effectively.",
      userId: 1,
    },
    headers: {
      "Authorization": "Bearer mock_token_123"
    }
  }).then((response) => {
    expect(response.status).to.eq(201);
    expect(response.body).to.have.property("id");
    expect(response.body.title).to.eq("Cypress API Testing");
  });
});"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Authentication"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common pattern in Cypress testing is to acquire an authentication token via cy.request() during the cy.session() or beforeEach() hook, and then inject it into subsequent requests or local storage."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"beforeEach(() => {
  cy.request({
    method: "POST",
    url: "/api/login",
    body: { username: "admin", password: "password123" }
  }).then((response) => {
    window.localStorage.setItem("jwt_token", response.body.token);
  });
});"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Error Handling"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, cy.request() automatically fails the test if the server responds with a 4xx or 5xx status code. If you want to test negative scenarios and handle error statuses explicitly, you can pass failOnStatusCode: false in the configuration object."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"it("Handles 404 errors gracefully", () => {
  cy.request({
    method: "GET",
    url: "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/non-existent-endpoint",
    failOnStatusCode: false
  }).then((response) => {
    expect(response.status).to.eq(404);
  });
});"}]
